=== Known false-positive vendor extraction (pre-existing, not fixed per scope discipline) ===
src/normalize.py:match_vendors() does plain substring matching on lowercased text, so
short vendor/category tokens in config/sources.yaml collide with ordinary English words:
  - "SEC" (regulatory-entity token) matches any occurrence of "sec" as a substring —
    "security", "insecure", "sector", "second", etc. Hit ~15 of the 84 items today, almost
    all false positives (e.g. item "phpIPAM... insecure object cache keying" tagged vendor=SEC).
  - "SSE" (category token) similarly false-matched at least one item with no SSE/SASE
    relevance (Konami Metal Gear Online 3 buffer overflow, item #57, tagged vendor=SSE).
  - "Hunters" (SIEM/security-data vendor) false-matched both ReliaQuest/ShinyHunters items
    (ShinyHunters contains "Hunters" as a substring) — coincidentally topically adjacent
    here (both are about the ShinyHunters group) but NOT actually about the vendor Hunters,
    so it was NOT counted as a vendor-of-interest match in scoring.
Manually discounted SEC/SSE/Hunters vendor tags throughout clustering and scoring below
unless the article body genuinely concerned that vendor/entity. Logging this for the user
to consider anchoring match_vendors() to word boundaries in a future change — out of scope
for this run.

=== Clustering ===
Ran a script implementing the four clustering rules (CVE, canonical URL, fuzzy title
token_set_ratio>=85 within 48h, shared vendor token within 24h) over all 84 items.
Raw result: 76 clusters. Two of those raw clusters are algorithmic over-merges caused by
generic tokens matching rule 4:
  - Item #6 (Microsoft Teams bot-blocking feature) merged with item #8 (Mirage2FA/M365
    phishing) purely because both share the vendor token "Microsoft" within 24h. Treated
    as two separate, unrelated stories editorially — Teams admin feature did not make the
    candidate pool for a top-5 pick (not commercially interesting enough on its own).
  - Item #13 (Oracle WebLogic KEV) merged with item #17 (Zimbra KEV) purely because both
    share the vendor token "CISA" within 24h. Treated as two separate stories — different
    vendor, different CVE, different product. Oracle WebLogic made the top 5; Zimbra did
    not (see "Notable drops" below).
This is a known limitation of rule 4 when clustering on regulatory/platform tokens that
appear across unrelated stories (CISA, Microsoft, SEC, SSE) — worth the user tightening
rule 4 to require a vendor token that isn't in a "generic" allowlist, or requiring 2+
shared tokens. Not fixed here per scope discipline.

